WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.514
Binary lifting precomputes the two-to-the-k-th ancestor of every tree node for k from zero up to log N.

00:00:07.514 --> 00:00:14.152
Jumping two to the k steps equals jumping two to the k minus one steps twice in succession.

00:00:14.152 --> 00:00:17.800
Filling the table takes O(N log N) time and space.

00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:24.918
Any jump distance k decomposes uniquely into a sum of powers of two via its set binary bits.

00:00:24.918 --> 00:00:32.272
Inspect each bit of k and advance u through the corresponding table entry whenever that bit is one.

00:00:32.272 --> 00:00:38.363
A query that would take N steps linearly finishes in at most twenty table lookups.

00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:46.462
To find the Lowest Common Ancestor of u and v, first lift the deeper node so both sit at the exact same depth.

00:00:46.462 --> 00:00:49.526
If they now coincide, that node is the LCA.

00:00:49.526 --> 00:00:59.785
Otherwise, iterate powers of two from largest to smallest and lift both pointers whenever their targets differ, stopping one step below the LCA.

00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:10.221
Once the Lowest Common Ancestor w is known, the shortest path between u and v climbs from u up to w and descends from w to v.

00:01:10.221 --> 00:01:16.002
Their edge distance equals depth[u] plus depth[v] minus twice depth[w].

00:01:16.002 --> 00:01:23.413
Weighted path totals work the same way by replacing depth with root-to-node prefix weights.
